---
term: Question of arbitration
aka:
  - The Question of Arbitration
section: premise
order: 10
definition: The weakness of decentralized mass movements in forming and enacting a clear agenda, which leaves them ineffective at achieving concrete political change.
seeAlso:
  - social-charter
  - vanguard-interest
  - big-tent
---
Decentralized movements gather many people, but they have no agreed way to turn many positions into one agenda. Members disagree about the path forward, each faction can block the others, and the movement doesn't act.

Modern democracies show the same problem at a larger scale. A change happens only when every party that can block it, called a *veto player*, prefers the change to the status quo. As the number of veto players grows and their positions drift apart, the set of acceptable changes shrinks, and the status quo stays in place by default.

A deadlock isn't necessarily complete disagreement. It's a common state that probably holds some agreement the parties could reconcile, which three failures keep them from reaching:

- Discovery: trades that would leave everyone better off stay hidden when positions are vague and bundled.
- Commitment: parties won't concede without assurance that others will deliver.
- Cost: negotiating through side channels is slow and expensive.

The Social Charter addresses all three failures with explicit charters and a designed mechanism at each [[meeting of interests]], which extract whatever agreement the deadlock holds. When no agreement exists, the framework shows that openly, and the conflict falls under the [[limit of arbitration]]. When a charter has used up the agreement it can extract, members can end it through [[dissolution]].
